cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use pcountr, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software

title: "pcountr: Interactive Stratigraphic Grain Counter with PCount Legacy Support"
version: 0.9.0
# date-released: set this to the tag date (YYYY-MM-DD) when you cut the release.
date-released: "2026-09-03"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/wnanavati/pcountr"
url: "https://github.com/wnanavati/pcountr"

# After the first Zenodo deposit, add the concept DOI here:
#   doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX
# It is omitted rather than stubbed so that GitHub's "Cite this repository"
# panel never renders a dead identifier.

authors:
  - family-names: Nanavati
    given-names: William
    email: william.nanavati@umontana.edu
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4853-5429"
    affiliation: "University of Montana"

abstract: >-
  An interactive, keystroke-driven counting application for recording
  stratigraphic assemblages at the microscope - pollen, diatoms, charcoal
  morphotypes, or any proxy counted by traversing a slide. Counts are saved
  continuously to a self-contained YAML format carrying identifications,
  metadata, depth and age. Provides report calculations (assemblage sums,
  tracer-spike ratios, concentrations, traverse statistics, preservation
  tabulations), rarefaction-based count targets, a wide samples-by-taxa matrix
  for stratigraphic plotting, accumulation rates, and export to Tilia XML for
  Neotoma integration. Also reads and converts legacy MS-DOS PCount .CNT files
  and their .DIC dictionaries. Dedicated to the memory of Dr. Eric C. Grimm
  (1951-2020), who developed PCount at the Illinois State Museum.

keywords:
  - palynology
  - paleoecology
  - paleolimnology
  - pollen
  - diatoms
  - microscopy
  - stratigraphy
  - Quaternary
  - Neotoma
  - R
  - shiny

references:
  - type: software
    title: "PCount"
    version: "2.0"
    authors:
      - family-names: Grimm
        given-names: "Eric C."
    year: 1994
    notes: >-
      MS-DOS software. Illinois State Museum, Research and Collections Center,
      Springfield, Illinois. The .CNT grammar, concentration equation and
      preservation scheme reimplemented in pcountr derive from PCount 2.0.
